WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:10.600
Disability Heritage:
Archaeology with Difference

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.940
This video tells you about
a museum exhibition titled

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:22.980
Disability Heritage:
Archaeology with Difference.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:33.660
It has been curated by Hannah Vogel,
an archaeologist and PhD researcher.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:40.460
Hannah’s researching disability
in history and archaeology.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:48.600
This exhibition is both an online
and physical museum display.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:55.920
The display is at the Gale History Museum
at Macquarie University, Sydney Australia

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.840
from November 2026 until March 2027.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.400
Online access exhibition
will remain available.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.900
In this video we will provide
an overview of exhibition,

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:16.160
and the accessibility features
incorporated to the display and website.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.160
The introductory panel reads;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:26.560
Disability Heritage:
Archaeology with Difference

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.300
Disability is part of our history.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:35.820
However, archaeology is often
ableist, overlooking,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:39.740
or worse, devaluing disabled experiences.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:44.460
This display invites you
to think differently.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.940
If we place disability at
the centre of our story,

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:56.800
the object on the top shelf can
represent the use of mobility aids,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.340
despite not usually being understood
as disability representation.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:06.900
The middle shelf challenges
the ideal of a bodily norm.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.300
It features a diversity of bodies,
both human and divine,

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:15.480
demonstrating difference across
cultures and throughout time.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:22.980
The feeding vessels on the bottom shelf
are evidence of community and care.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:28.560
These objects, created
thousands of years apart,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:36.160
are products of differences in
bodily experience within a community.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:43.220
This display is not only about disability,
it is for the disability community.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:49.180
The accessibility ‘features’
are disability heritage just as

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:53.480
much as the archaeological
material behind the glass.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:58.080
In the museum display,
the wall panel includes:

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.020
A tactile fabrication
of scene on the first object,

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:06.640
3D prints of objects on
the second and third shelves

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:13.900
A QR code and an ipad to watch
this video or access this website.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:19.160
We also have nearby a Braille
and Large-print guide

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:22.340
of the panel text and labels.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.680
For our website, alongside this video,

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:33.120
we also have interactive 3D
models, customisable text size,

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.480
audio-visual descriptions
and high-resolution photographs

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.100
with alternative text descriptions,

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:45.080
which you can zoom in or explore
the artefacts from multiple perspectives.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.120
In the display itself,
there are three shelves.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.740
The top shelf has one object, a pelike.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:04:00.380
This pelike depicts the use
of a mobility aid: a staff or cane.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:06.020
In ancient Greece, staff-like
objects were also symbols of power,

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:08.000
authority and justice.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:15.980
Archaeological narratives are selective
about how these artefacts are interpreted,

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.360
often rejecting the possibility

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:21.820
of disability coinciding
with symbols of power.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:24.820
Pelike

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:30.300
This Greek vase shows two young men.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:36.260
The man on the left leans on a long
staff for support, like a cane.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:43.240
On the middle shelve there are seven
small figurines or amulets.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:48.580
Figurines were made for various reasons
and were often accessible to many people.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:05:00.120
These differently shaped bodies are votive
offerings or amulets used for protection,

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:10.560
ensuring health and/or fertility, invoking
the divine, or for afterlife beliefs.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.880
These are an important
reflection of the reality

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.200
of diversity, rather than an ‘ideal’ body.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.360
Reclining figure

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.840
A small clay figure of a person
lying on their side on a couch.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:31.380
It still has traces of its original
pink, cream and yellow paint.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.280
Bronze dancer (replica)

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:42.440
A modern copy
of an ancient Etruscan bronze

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:46.360
of a woman in motion, arms held out wide.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:52.420
The original is at
the Museum of Fine Arts in Boston.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.780
Figure of a musician

00:05:55.780 --> 00:06:02.520
A clay figure from ancient Greece
of a musician kneeling and playing a drum,

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.240
with some of its blue
and black paint still there.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.840
Seated statuette

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:21.040
The oldest object in the display,
from Mesopotamia, around 3000 BCE:

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.940
a person sitting upright in a chair,
wearing a tall headdress.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:32.500
Bronze figure of Priapus

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:41.420
A Roman bronze of the god Priapus,
shown with a very large penis.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:47.960
Even a god's body is shown
as different, not ‘ideal’.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:51.480
Terracotta figurine

00:06:51.480 --> 00:07:00.420
A simple clay figure from Cyprus,
shaped by pinching and folding the clay,

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:04.900
with a long nose and a wide, flared body.

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:09.780
Amulet of Pataikos

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:18.900
A tiny Egyptian amulet of the god
Pataikos, shown as a man with dwarfism.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:24.060
The loop on the back means it
may have been worn on a string.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.160
The bottom shelf has three objects.

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:32.380
These feeding vessels, despite
differences in design over time,

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:35.500
were made to support the needs of people.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:40.380
They represent difference creating
change in material culture.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:42.880
Feeding cup

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:47.600
A porcelain feeding cup from 1930.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:55.280
The long spout and half-covered
top let it be tilted for drinking,

00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:01.360
and the worn gold line
suggests it was used often.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:04.920
Spouted vessel

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:13.640
A Mycenaean jug from
around 1400 to 1200 BCE.

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:18.320
Its spout points upward
so it can be tilted to pour,

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:21.000
made to support someone’s needs.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:23.660
Feeding vessel

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:27.880
A small feeding vessel
from ancient Athens,

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:34.360
with a thin spout and a closed
top full of little holes.

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:37.480
This project has included many members

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:43.280
of the disability community throughout
all stages of its development.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:47.080
Acknowledgments
are featured on the website.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:54.220
This exhibition is funded by the
National Disability Research Partnership’s

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:59.860
inaugural
Disability Research Leadership Program

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:09.380
and is part of Hannah’s PhD project
supported by the Commonwealth

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:13.080
through an Australian Government
Research Training Program.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:20.300
If you want to learn more about
Hannah Vogel’s research or other projects,

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:26.900
you can find her profiles
on the website’s About page.
